    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> founded Karakorum in 1220 in a valley that earlier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gokturk-khaganate">Göktürk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghur</a> empires had used, and under Ögedei from 1235 it became a walled capital with a fixed palace.</li><li>It was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a> until 1260, when the empire divided and Khubilai Khan made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dadu">Dadu</a> the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a>, leaving Karakorum an administrative centre.</li><li>Ming armies occupied it in 1372 and 1380, and in 1585 its stones and bricks went into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erdene-zuu">Erdene Zuu Monastery</a>; its site was identified in 1889.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>A large tree of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and other precious metals stood in the courtyard of the Khan's palace, with four golden serpents round the trunk and a mechanical angel that sounded a trumpet when drinks were wanted. It was designed by Guillaume Boucher, an enslaved Parisian goldsmith.</p></aside>
    </section>

