    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1220</span><span class="rf-text">The foundation of Karakorum is usually dated to this year</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1235</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ogedei-khan">Ögedei Khan</a> decrees the Palace of Myriad Peace and has city walls built</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1254</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-of-rubruck">William of Rubruck</a> reaches Karakorum</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1260</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a> divides and Khubilai Khan develops <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dadu">Dadu</a> as the capital of the Yuan Empire</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1307</span><span class="rf-text">Lingbei Province is founded and Karakorum remains the administrative centre north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gobi-desert">Gobi</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1372</span><span class="rf-text">A Ming army under Li Wenzhong occupies the city and causes severe damage</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1585</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abtai-sain-khan">Abtai Sain Khan</a> founds the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erdene-zuu">Erdene Zuu Monastery</a> on the palace site, using stones and bricks of Karakorum</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1889</span><span class="rf-text">Nikolai Yadrintsev identifies the ruins as the Mongol capital</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ordu-baliq"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ordu-baliq.jpg" alt="Ordu-Baliq" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ordu-baliq">Ordu-Baliq</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ordu-baliq.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">James A. Glazier, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Orkhon valley was a centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gokturk-khaganate">Göktürk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghur</a> empires. To the Göktürks the nearby Khangai Mountains were the location of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otuken">Ötüken</a>, the locus of power, and the Uyghur capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ordu-baliq">Karabalgasun</a> stood 27 km north-west of where Karakorum was later built. The area is also considered one of the oldest farming areas in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>.</p><p>In 1218 and 1219 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> rallied his troops for the campaign against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazmian-empire">Khwarazmian Empire</a> at a place called Karakorum, but the foundation of a city is usually said to have occurred only in 1220. Until 1235 Karakorum seems to have been little more than a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yurt">yurt</a> town. The site had several advantages. It was more central to the growing empire, and the climate near the end of the Medieval Warm Period was warmer and moister than today, which gave abundant grass for grazing. The Orkhon flowed north to south in a region where most rivers run east to west, which eased travel by river and gave a point of control over the natural network. The place had also been the centre of power of earlier empires, and this expressed Mongol control over the largely non-Mongol population of the region. An older layer from the 8th century has been found under the 13th-century walls, and it has been theorised to be the Takhai Balgas mentioned in Mongolian chronicles.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mongke"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mongke.jpg" alt="Möngke" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mongke">Möngke</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:M%C3%B6ngke_enthroned._Tarikh-i_Jahangushay-i_Juvaini,_1438,_Shiraz.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1438 artist, Shiraz, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Only in 1235, after the defeat of the Jin empire, did Ögedei raise city walls and build a fixed palace. He decreed the Tumen Amgalan Ord, the Palace of Myriad Peace, in 1235, and it was finished in one year. The History of Yuan records its establishment in 1236. Twice a year the khan stayed in the city for an extended period, and Ögedei encouraged the elite of the empire to build houses near his palace in order to centralise his power over the empire.</p><p>Under Ögedei and his successors Karakorum became a major site for world politics. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongke">Möngke Khan</a> had the palace enlarged and the great stupa temple completed. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">Silver</a> Tree was made for the centre of the city by Guillaume Boucher.</p><p>After 1260 the city declined in rank, but in 1299 it was expanded eastwards. In the first half of the 14th century a series of public buildings was erected, to which inscription stones bear witness, and the stupa temples were renewed in 1311 and again from 1342 to 1346. The first half of the 14th century was a new era of prosperity for the city.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/yaroslav-ii-of-vladimir"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yaroslav-ii-of-vladimir.jpg" alt="Yaroslav II of Vladimir" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/yaroslav-ii-of-vladimir">Yaroslav II of Vladimir</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yaroslav_II_Vsevolodovich_grand_duke_of_Vladimir.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Karakorum was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a> between 1235 and 1260. Foreign rulers came to it: Russian princes usually went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarai">Sarai</a>, the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>, to have their right to the throne confirmed, but sometimes had to travel to Karakorum. After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslav-ii-of-vladimir">Yaroslav Vsevolodovich</a> in 1246, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batu-khan">Batu Khan</a> granted the patent for the throne of Vladimir to his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sviatoslav-vsevolodovich">Sviatoslav</a>, but the conflict between Batu Khan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guyuk-khan">Güyük Khan</a> led to competing appointments. Yaroslav's eldest sons Alexander and Andrey were summoned to Karakorum, where the grand princely throne was given to Andrey.</p><p>After the dissolution of the empire in 1260 into the Yuan Empire, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a>, the Chaghatayid Khanate and the Golden Horde, Khubilai Khan developed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dadu">Dadu</a>, present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, as the capital of the Yuan Empire. In 1271 Karakorum was reduced to an administrative centre of a provincial backwater of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a>. It kept a high ideological status as one of the camps of the Chinggisids, and after the foundation of Lingbei Province in 1307 it remained the administrative centre of the region north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gobi-desert">Gobi Desert</a>.</p><p>After the collapse of the Yuan in 1368, Karakorum became the residence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayushiridara">Biligtü Khan</a> in 1370, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-yuan">Northern Yuan</a> used it as a capital. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dayan-khan">Dayan Khan</a> made it a capital once again at the beginning of the 16th century.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-of-rubruck">William of Rubruck</a>, a Flemish Franciscan missionary and papal envoy, left one of the most detailed accounts of the city, though not always a flattering one. He compared it unfavourably with the village of Saint-Denis near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and thought the royal abbey there ten times as magnificent as the Khan's palace. He also described the town as very cosmopolitan and religiously tolerant. The walled city had four gates facing the four directions, two quarters of fixed houses, one for the "Saracenes" and one for the "Cathai", twelve pagan temples, two mosques and a Nestorian church.</p><p>The palace was built in the Chinese architectural style, with three main buildings side by side. Rubruck described its hall as like a church, with a nave and two rows of pillars, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">Silver</a> Tree standing inside the middle door and the khan seated on a high place to the north. The non-palatial part of the settlement grew only to the north of the palace, in the manner of the traditional circular camp, which left the khan clear access to the river and the forested mountains to the south. Apart from the permanent palace there was a moving palace that settled in a ring on its stops. According to Juvayni, Ögedei invited people into the open yard of the treasure buildings and let them carry away all they could in a set time, and he gave frequent donations from the treasure to the city's poor.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ariq-boke"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ariq-boke.jpg" alt="Ariq Böke" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ariq-boke">Ariq Böke</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ariq_B%C3%B6ke.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Miskin, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The division of the empire hurt the city. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toluid-civil-war">Toluid Civil War</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ariq-boke">Ariq Böke</a>, Khubilai disrupted the town's grain supply in 1260. In 1277 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaidu">Kaidu</a> took Karakorum, and Yuan troops and Bayan of the Baarin ousted him the following year.</p><p>After the fall of the Yuan, Ming armies struck the city. In 1372 a Ming army under General Li Wenzhong occupied Karakorum and caused severe damage. In 1380 Ming troops occupied it again and later razed it. The permanent palace was destroyed by the Ming in 1388, although a moving palace remained in the region until 1585.</p><p>According to Saghang Sechen's Erdeni-yin Tobči, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurultai">kurultai</a> decided in 1415 to rebuild the city, but no archaeological evidence of such a venture has been found. Karakorum was inhabited at the beginning of the 16th century, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dayan-khan">Dayan Khan</a> made it a capital again. In the following years the town changed hands between Oirads and Chinggisids several times and was then given up permanently.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/erdene-zuu"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/erdene-zuu.jpg" alt="Erdene Zuu" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/erdene-zuu">Erdene Zuu</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%C5%9Awi%C4%85tynia_Zachodnia_w_klasztorze_Erdene_Dzuu_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Marcin Konsek, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 1585 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abtai-sain-khan">Abtai Sain Khan</a>, the senior Genghisid of the central Mongolian region, restored the area of the permanent palace as a monastery, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erdene-zuu">Erdene Zuu</a>, using the stones and bricks of Karakorum. Fragments of the Sino-Mongolian inscription of 1342 are built into its walls. Erdene Zuu is likely the oldest surviving Buddhist monastery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> and became the mother monastery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulaanbaatar">Urga</a>, present-day Ulaanbaatar, which Tusheet Khan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gombodorj">Gombodorj</a> founded in 1639.</p><p>The location of the city was long unclear. Its identification with Erdene Zuu was hinted at in the 18th century, and there was a dispute into the 20th century over whether the ruins of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ordu-baliq">Karabalgasun</a> were those of Karakorum. In 1889 Nikolai Yadrintsev conclusively identified the site as the Mongol capital, and Wilhelm Radloff confirmed his conclusion. The first excavations took place in 1933 and 1934 under D. Bukinich. Sergei Kiselyov concluded from his work of 1948 and 1949 that he had found Ögödei's palace, but the German-Mongolian excavations of 2000 to 2004 seem to identify the remains as the great stupa temple. The finds include paved roads, brick and adobe buildings, floor heating systems, the working of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>, gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, iron and glass, four kilns, and ceramics and coins from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>.</p><p>The ruins lie beside the present town of Kharkhorin in the upper part of the Orkhon Valley <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>. In 2023 the President of Mongolia announced a plan to rebuild the historical capital.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Karakorum was founded in 1220 in the Orkhon valley and was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a> from 1235 to 1260. After the empire divided it became a provincial centre, and Ming armies damaged it in 1372 and 1380. Its stones went into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erdene-zuu">Erdene Zuu Monastery</a>.</em></p>

